# ssh-google-auth — TOTP 2FA for SSH

Add a time-based one-time-password (TOTP) second factor to SSH logins using
Google Authenticator and PAM.

## Install (openSUSE Tumbleweed)

```sh
sudo zypper install google-authenticator-libpam
```

## Run the enrollment wizard as the user who will log in

```sh
google-authenticator
```

Answer `y` to time-based tokens, updating your `~/.google_authenticator`,
disallowing token reuse, and enabling rate-limiting. Save the emergency
scratch codes it prints somewhere safe.

## Enable the PAM module for SSH

```sh
# /etc/pam.d/sshd — add near the top
auth required pam_google_authenticator.so
```

## Turn on challenge-response in sshd

```sh
# /etc/ssh/sshd_config
KbdInteractiveAuthentication yes
# older builds call this:
# ChallengeResponseAuthentication yes
```

## Restart sshd

```sh
sudo systemctl restart sshd
```

## Add the account to the phone app

Open Google Authenticator → add account → **Enter a setup key** → paste the
secret key from the wizard, choose **Time based**, save.

> Log in from another box. You'll be asked for your password (or key) and then
> the 6-digit verification code from the app.
